@module flutter-m19 | Production and Publishing
@summary Prepare, sign, build and ship apps to Google Play and keep them healthy.

@lesson flutter-m19-l01 | App icons | 8
## Learning objectives
- Generate adaptive icons

## App icons
Use `flutter_launcher_icons` to generate Android adaptive icons and iOS icons from one source image.

```dart
// flutter_launcher_icons:
//   android: true
//   ios: true
//   image_path: assets/icon.png
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Provide adaptive and monochrome icons.

@lesson flutter-m19-l02 | Splash screens | 8
## Learning objectives
- Configure launch screens

## Splash screens
Android 12+ uses the system SplashScreen API. `flutter_native_splash` generates native splash resources for all platforms.

```dart
// flutter_native_splash:
//   color: '#6750F5'
//   image: assets/splash.png
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Keep splash screens brief.

@lesson flutter-m19-l03 | Android signing | 8
## Learning objectives
- Sign release builds

## Android signing
Create an upload keystore with keytool, reference it from `key.properties`, configure `signingConfigs` in Gradle, and never commit the keystore.

```dart
// keytool -genkey -v -keystore upload-keystore.jks -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 -alias upload
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Back up your upload key securely.

@lesson flutter-m19-l04 | App bundles | 8
## Learning objectives
- Build Android App Bundles

## App bundles
Google Play requires `.aab` bundles; Play generates optimised APKs per device.

```dart
// flutter build appbundle --release
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Upload AAB files to Play.

@lesson flutter-m19-l05 | APK generation | 8
## Learning objectives
- Build APKs for testing

## APK generation
APKs are useful for direct installs and testers. `--split-per-abi` makes smaller APKs.

```dart
// flutter build apk --release --split-per-abi
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Use APKs for side-loading and testing.

@lesson flutter-m19-l06 | Google Play Console | 8
## Learning objectives
- Create and configure your app

## Google Play Console
In Play Console you create the app, complete the content rating, data safety form, target audience and app access, then upload releases.

```dart
// Play Console → Create app → Dashboard tasks
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Complete all policy declarations.

@lesson flutter-m19-l07 | Store listing | 8
## Learning objectives
- Write a listing that converts

## Store listing
Provide a clear title, short and full description, high-quality screenshots, a feature graphic and an accurate category.

```dart
// Title ≤ 30 chars, short description ≤ 80 chars
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Screenshots sell the app.

@lesson flutter-m19-l08 | Privacy policy | 8
## Learning objectives
- Publish a privacy policy

## Privacy policy
Play requires a privacy policy URL describing data collection, use, sharing and deletion, consistent with the Data safety form.

```dart
// Host the policy on a public URL and link it in the app
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Keep the policy accurate and accessible.

@lesson flutter-m19-l09 | App permissions | 8
## Learning objectives
- Request only what you need

## App permissions
Declare minimal permissions; sensitive ones (location in background, SMS) need justification and review.

```dart
// <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Fewer permissions, more trust.

@lesson flutter-m19-l10 | Version management | 8
## Learning objectives
- Version every release

## Version management
Increase the build number for every upload (`version: 1.3.0+14`) and follow semantic versioning for the name.

```dart
// version: 1.3.0+14
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Build numbers must always increase.

@lesson flutter-m19-l11 | Release channels | 8
## Learning objectives
- Roll out safely

## Release channels
Use internal, closed and open testing tracks, then staged rollouts to production (e.g. 10% → 50% → 100%).

```dart
// Internal → Closed → Open → Production (staged)
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Staged rollouts limit risk.

@lesson flutter-m19-l12 | Crash monitoring | 8
## Learning objectives
- Watch app health after release

## Crash monitoring
Monitor Crashlytics and Play Console Android vitals (crash and ANR rates) and halt rollouts if they spike.

```dart
// Halt a staged rollout from Play Console if crash rate rises
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Act quickly on vitals.

@lesson flutter-m19-l13 | CI/CD | 8
## Learning objectives
- Automate builds and releases

## CI/CD
Use GitHub Actions, Codemagic or Bitrise to run analyze, tests and builds on every push, and fastlane or Play APIs to upload releases.

```dart
// - run: flutter analyze
// - run: flutter test
// - run: flutter build appbundle
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Automate checks on every change.

@lesson flutter-m19-l14 | Updates and maintenance | 8
## Learning objectives
- Keep the app current

## Updates and maintenance
Update Flutter and dependencies regularly, meet Play's target API deadlines, and use in-app update prompts for critical fixes.

```dart
// flutter upgrade && flutter pub upgrade
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Plan regular maintenance releases.
